+### Parameters
+*   **Number of Gaussians:** Typically 200,000–500,000 for complex scenes [S1].
+*   **Spherical Harmonics Degree:** Degree 3 is standard, resulting in 16 coefficients per color channel (48 total floats) [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Tile Size:** Commonly $16\times16$ pixels for balancing parallelism and memory bandwidth [S1, G].
+*   **Alpha Threshold:** Rendering stops when transmittance $T < 10^{-4}$ [G].
 
 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
 *   **Aliasing:** High-frequency content can cause aliasing artifacts. 3DGS uses a dilation-based filter to mitigate this, but it may fail with varying focal lengths or camera distances [S1].
-*   **Covariance Validity:** Directly updating the entries of the covariance matrix $\Sigma$ during training does not guarantee it remains positive semi-definite. Using the scale and rotation parameterization ensures $\Sigma$ is always valid [S2, unofficial].
-*   **View-Dependent Color:** If spherical harmonics are not used (e.g., in some volume rendering adaptations), the scene appears dimmer and less vibrant because it lacks viewpoint-dependent color shifts [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Covariance Validity:** Directly updating the covariance matrix entries during training can break the positive semi-definite property. Using scale and rotation parameters guarantees a valid covariance matrix [S2, unofficial].
+*   **View-Dependent Color:** If spherical harmonics are not used (isotropic volumes), the rendered scene appears dimmer and less vibrant, as it lacks viewpoint-dependent color shifts [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Lighting Limitations:** The emission-absorption model bakes lighting into SH coefficients, limiting performance on highly reflective surfaces and preventing dynamic relighting [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Surface Normal Rendering via External Channel](2099-surface-normal-rendering-via-external-channel.md)
-Enables rendering of surface normals from 3D Gaussian splats by treating normals as an auxiliary attribute, bypassing the lack of native geometric surface information in volumetric Gaussian ellipsoids.
+Renders surface normals by treating them as an extra attribute in an external channel, modifying the Gaussian's internal attribute representation rather than relying on external gradient supervision. Choose this when explicit normal maps are required for downstream geometry processing.
+
+### [Learnable Normals Parameterization](9615-learnable-normals-parameterization.md)
+(Sibling) Optimizes normals as part of the Gaussian parameters. Choose this when normal consistency is critical for training stability.
